 "limitations": [
  "The published accuracy is NOT reproduced. Under genuine leave-one-participant-out validation the retrained model has an overall 60 s RMSE of 0.765 MET (paper: 0.281); sedentary / light / moderate-vigorous 0.317 / 1.274 / 1.364 (paper: 0.268 / 0.307 / 0.310). On windows that really are non-sedentary the random forest reaches 1.31 MET RMSE for unseen people against 1.50 for predicting the training mean (explained variance about 24%); even knowing the activity leaves 0.80. The published LOSO loop in in-lab.py fits the classifier on all participants and calls a regression() helper that is not in the public repository, so the paper's figures could not be traced to code (see loso.w60_leakage_diagnostics for what leaking the held-out participant does).",
  "Under LOSO the retrained classifier separates sedentary from non-sedentary windows well (accuracy 0.973; sedentary-positive precision / recall / F1 0.985 / 0.975 / 0.980 against 0.952 / 0.628 / 0.758 in the paper). Alternative label and metric definitions that were tried did not reproduce the paper's lower recall.",
  "26 participants with obesity (BMI 30.54-46.23, age 25-64), scripted lab activities, a Fossil Sport Gen 4 watch on the dominant wrist. Younger or leaner people, free-living behaviour and non-dominant-wrist wear are outside the training distribution (ood_demographic / ood_signal quantify this).",
  "The WHOOP strap's axes are assumed to equal the training watch's axes (AxisMap identity). The training frame was measured (frame_reference): with the arm hanging, the window-mean accelerometer vector is ~(+8.8, -3.9, 0) m/s^2, i.e. +x runs along the forearm towards the elbow. A wrong mapping is only partly detectable (ood.stress_tests_on_training_windows).",
  "The regression stage is fit on the non-sedentary windows of the training participants only; the random forest cannot extrapolate beyond the MET values it saw (largest measured window MET in the training data: 7.6).",
  "Not a medical device; research use only."
 ],
